COMMAND NAME: gpssh-exkeys

Exchanges SSH public keys between hosts.


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpssh-exkeys { -f <host_file> | - h <hostname> [-h <hostname> ...] }

gpssh-exkeys -? | --version


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The gpssh-exkeys utility will exchange SSH keys between the 
specified hosts. This allows SSH connections between hosts without 
a password prompt. By default, keys will be exchanged as the 
currently logged in user. 

To specify the hosts involved in the SSH key exchange, use the 
-f option to specify a file containing a list of host names, or use 
the -h option to name single host names on the command-line. At 
least one host name (-h) or a host file (-f) is required. Note that 
the current host is included in the key exchange by default.

Greenplum recommends performing the key exchange process twice: 
once as root and once as the gpadmin user (the user designated 
to own your Greenplum Database installation). The Greenplum Database 
management scripts require that the same non-root user be created on 
all hosts in the Greenplum Database system, and the scripts must 
be able to connect as that user to all hosts without a password prompt.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-f <host_file>

Specifies the name of a file that contains a list of hosts that 
will participate in the SSH key exchange. The host name is required, 
and you can optionally specify an alternate user name and/or SSH port 
number per host. The syntax of the host file is one host per line 
as follows:
[username@]hostname[:ssh_port]


-h <hostname>

Specifies a single host name that will participate in the SSH 
key exchange. You can use the -h option multiple times to 
specify multiple host names.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.

--version

Displays the version of this utility.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Exchange SSH keys between all hosts listed in the file host_file:

gpssh-exkeys -f host_file


Exchange SSH keys between the hosts thumper0, thumper1, and 
thumper2 as the currently logged in user:

gpssh-exkeys -h sdw1 -h sdw2 -h sdw3


